<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.7.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01374</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.7.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>06a584b2-3941-48ca-b143-97e8a7202332</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="485b1684-c20e-4909-ad09-92211faca10b" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
  <!--Multiple Choice Starts here-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a5ee2468-69c0-4130-985e-a3f0c94a4142" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="The algae bloom represented by the triangles because the curve has a higher \(y\)-intercept." data-solution-options="[&quot;The algae bloom represented by the triangles because the curve has a higher \\(y\\)-intercept.&quot;, &quot;The algae bloom represented by the circles because the curve has a lower \\(y\\)-intercept.&quot;, &quot;Both algae blooms covered the same area after 5 weeks.&quot;, &quot;The algae bloom represented by the circles because the curve is always below the other one on the graph.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The two graphs show models characterized by exponential decay representing the area covered by two different algae blooms, in square yards, \(w\) weeks after different chemicals were applied.
          
          <para><figure><media alt="Graph of two functions on grid."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/01d535fe6e3553e33a9ca3cfd066121d4051f44d"/></media></figure></para>
          <para>Which algae bloom covered a larger area when the chemicals were applied?</para>
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! The algae bloom represented by the triangles because it has a higher \(y\)-intercept.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="08f2e010-a77c-447b-a9b7-796d79040bf5" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="The algae bloom represented by the triangles because its graph is steeper." data-solution-options="[&quot;The algae bloom represented by the triangles because the curve has a higher \\(y\\)-intercept.&quot;, &quot;Both algae blooms are decreasing at the same rate.&quot;, &quot;The algae bloom represented by the circles because the curve is always below the other one on the graph.&quot;, &quot;The algae bloom represented by the triangles because its graph is steeper.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The two graphs show models characterized by exponential decay representing the area covered by two different algae blooms, in square yards, \(w\) weeks after different chemicals were applied.
          
          <para><figure><media alt="Graph of two functions on grid."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/01d535fe6e3553e33a9ca3cfd066121d4051f44d"/></media></figure></para>
          <para>Which algae population is decreasing more rapidly? Explain how you know.</para>
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The algae bloom represented by the triangles because the curves graph is steeper. </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a89b1e97-30fc-428f-baba-62b0d7a9180b" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(\frac34\) or \(.75\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(\\frac12\\) or \\(.5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(\\frac34\\) or \\(.75\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(\\frac14\\) or \\(.25\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(\\frac23\\) or \\(.66\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A medicine is applied to a burn on a patient’s arm. The area of the burn in square centimeters decreases exponentially and is shown in the graph.
          
          <para><figure><media alt="Graph of a function on grid."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dc81580bf31268ae041f21941206e049417ece5b"/></media></figure></para>
          <para>What fraction of the burn area remains each week?</para>
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! .75 or \(\frac34\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.1. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="dd028626-75d1-412e-80f9-2bb42d1333b5" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(a= 8 (\frac{3}{4})^t\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(a= 8 (\\frac{1}{4})^t\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(a= 8 (\\frac{3}{4})^t\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(a = -2t+8\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(a= \\frac{-6}{5}t+8\\)  &quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A medicine is applied to a burn on a patient’s arm. The area of the burn in square centimeters decreases exponentially and is shown in the graph.
          
          <para><figure><media alt="Graph of a function on grid."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dc81580bf31268ae041f21941206e049417ece5b"/></media></figure></para>
          <para>Which of the following is an equation representing the area of the burn, \(a\), after \(t\) weeks?</para>
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(a= 8 (\frac{3}{4})^t\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="816acdc7-11e3-4a82-8f9a-c533c67578ad" data-problem-comparator="text" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="1.068">
    <problem>
      <para>A medicine is applied to a burn on a patient’s arm. The area of the burn in square centimeters decreases exponentially and is shown in the graph.
          
          <para><figure><media alt="Graph of a function on grid."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dc81580bf31268ae041f21941206e049417ece5b"/></media></figure></para>
          <para>What is the area of the burn after 7 weeks? Round to three decimal places.</para>
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 1.068</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="558b3142-705a-46fe-bd11-b132fffc0fc8" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\( m=200(\frac{7}{10})^h\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(m=500(\\frac{3}{10})^h\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(m=500(\\frac{7}{10})^h\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(m=200(\\frac{3}{10})^h\\)&quot;, &quot;\\( m=200(\\frac{7}{10})^h\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The graphs show the amounts of medicine in two patients after receiving injections. The circles show the medicine in patient A, and the triangles show the medicine in patient B.
          
          <para><figure><media alt="Two functions plotted on the same grid."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ddb38359db9104f960039e209c5c41e077450be0"/></media></figure></para>
          <para>One equation that gives the amount of medicine in milligrams, \(m\), in patient A, \(h\) hours after an injection, is \(m=300(\frac{1}{2})^h\).</para>
          <para>What could be an equation for the amount of medicine in patient B?</para>
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \( m=200(\frac{7}{10})^h\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q7-->
  <exercise data-content-id="c0ce2bd6-b9f8-487b-8877-095fa40740a4" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="A population of bacteria doubles every hour and starts with 10 bacteria." data-solution-options="[&quot;A population of bacteria doubles every hour and starts with 10 bacteria.&quot;, &quot;A savings account starts with $200 and decays by a factor of \\(\\frac12\\) each month.&quot;, &quot;A population of bacteria increases by a factor of 10 every hour and starts with 2 bacteria.&quot;, &quot;A population of bacteria starts with 200 and decays by a factor of \\(\\frac12\\) every hour.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which scenario could describe the graph?

          <para><figure><media alt="GRAPH OF AN INCREASING EXPONENTIAL FUNCTION WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF 10 AND PASSING THROUGH THE POINTS (1, 20) AND (2, 40)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/818c174ee5b2dcd9969f882b192054c6cd590336"/></media></figure></para>
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! A population of bacteria doubles every hour and starts with 10 bacteria.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q8-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a1f8b0b4-7b47-4e6b-9bc9-229400a0148c" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(y= 2.104(1.479)^x\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(y= 2.104(1.479)^x\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y= 2.5x+2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y= 2.104(.479)^x\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=.479(2.104)^x\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which equation could represent the graph?
          <para><figure><media alt="SCATTER PLOT THAT CAN BE MODELED BY AN INCREASING EXPONENTIAL FUNCTION WITH A \(y\)-intercepts THAT IS SLIGHTLY MORE THAN 2. THE SCATTER PLOT INCLUDES A POINT WITH AN X-VALUE OF 1 AND A Y-VALUE OF APPROXIMATELY 3-POINT-1, A POINT WITH AN X-VALUE OF 2 AND A Y-VALUE OF APPROXIMATELY 4-POINT-6, AND A POINT WITH AN X-VALUE OF 3 AND A Y-VALUE OF APPROXIMATELY 6-POINT-8."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/5d7262afbf8ac82db2414db4c0f8f2ad57978019"/></media></figure></para>
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q9-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1b655f00-f67d-49bb-9eca-e83983e2de0d" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="Yes, if the growth rate is between zero and one it’s decay; if the growth rate is greater than one it is growth." data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes, if the initial value is greater than one it’s growth; if the initial value is less than one it’s decay.&quot;, &quot;Yes, if the growth rate is between zero and one it’s decay; if the growth rate is greater than one it is growth.&quot;, &quot;Yes, if the growth rate is greater than one it’s growth; if the growth rate is, less than zero it’s decay.&quot;, &quot;No, it is not possible to determine growth and decay from the formula.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Given a formula for an exponential function, is it possible to determine whether the function grows or decays exponentially just by looking at the formula? Explain. </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response"> </div>
</div>
  </section>
